WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.739
If you're on hold to a company, and they're
playing music down the phone at you,

00:00:03.739 --> 00:00:08.260
the chances are it sounds terrible, and sometimes
it just dissolves into static.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.610
Telephone signals are not designed to carry
music, and to explain why,

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.539
we have to start way, way back
with old telephone equipment like this.

00:00:15.539 --> 00:00:19.210
Early telephone calls were put through by
a human operator

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.500
who would be literally connecting the two
phone wires into one circuit

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.350
at a big board of switches.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:25.730
Or a "switchboard".

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:29.270
Any sound at one end,
whether it's speech, music, whatever,

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.660
would be converted to an electrical signal,
sent down the wire,

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.750
and converted back at the other end.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.270
You send music, you receive music --

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:41.150
although it'd be limited by the abilities
of the tiny microphone and speaker.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.980
But telephone exchanges like this only work
for small numbers of connections.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.630
Because, you know,
you can't do this for every call.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:48.720
[PHONE RINGS]

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.470
Phone companies quickly realised
that they could save

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:55.000
on setting up thousands of wires
between exchanges miles apart

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.500
if they were able to put several conversations
down one wire at the same time.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.210
So rather than transmit every frequency of
sound in the original signal,

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.570
they would cut off the high and low frequencies,

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:09.250
the ones we don't absolutely need to understand
speech,

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.850
and then they would shift each call into a
different frequency band.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.720
At at the other end, like tuning in a radio,

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.640
they would shift each call back to the right
frequencies so you could hear it.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.619
I mean, the quality wasn't great, but it was
good enough.

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:25.800
And hey, we got cheap long distance calling.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.400
We got used to that phone-quality sound.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.500
[EXCHANGE CLICKS]

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.220
And it became the standard.

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:33.740
So already [PHONE RINGS] music was going to
sound a bit dodgy,

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.130
with all the high and low
frequencies cut off.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.320
But while we were still running
on analogue circuits

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:39.360
[EXCHANGE RESETS]

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.970
it wasn't going to sound like static.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.369
That was the fault of computers.

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:48.550
Phone companies realised that they could send
even more calls down the same wire,

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.759
or into the same airspace on a cellular network,

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:53.159
if they used computers to compress the audio.

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:55.480
And the more they compressed it,

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.439
the more calls they could fit
at the same time,

00:01:57.439 --> 00:01:59.600
and the cheaper it'd be for them.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.369
These days, there is enough power
in pretty much everyone's phone

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:07.030
to compress 4K video in real time,
but back then, not so much.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.190
MP3s had only just been invented,
and these do not have enough power

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.620
to compress those.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.770
The standards for phone calls, even today,
rely on quick-and-dirty compression

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:19.000
built decades ago, designed to run
on very little computing power,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.870
and optimised for speech.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:24.290
That compression is great at encoding one
clear voice --

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.150
but chuck a load of complicated music at it,
with all its instruments

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.470
[COMPRESSED MUSIC]
and it's going to sound bad.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.730
But not quite enough to break down into static.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.659
There is one last piece of the puzzle.

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:34.709
Someone else cutting corners.

00:02:34.709 --> 00:02:38.130
Because in the worst case, that hold music
isn’t just compressed

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.560
by the phone company on its way to you.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.120
It’s already been compressed
by a completely different system

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.900
as your call bounces around a company’s
internal phone network.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.250
An internal phone network that has
probably been built by the lowest bidder.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.400
So the phone company takes
that already-mangled signal

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.620
and mangles it in a different way
as it travels to you.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.349
And in a really badly designed system,

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:03.720
maybe there’s an entire third layer
of terrible compression

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.959
from whichever other company
runs the call centre

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:08.070
that you're actually talking to,
off in some other country.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:11.080
[MUSIC RISES]
Speech can survive that chain

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:12.530
But music...

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:16.080
[MUSIC TURNS TO STATIC]
...music can't!

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.950
Thank you so much to all the team
at the Milton Keynes Museum

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.440
and their Connected Earth gallery,

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.660
where pretty much all this
telephone exchange equipment

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.830
is just open to play around with.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.160
Pull down the description for more about them.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.380
This is not a TARDIS.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.680
This is literally just
a police telephone box.

